  ('118d7c80-306c-5248-8d64-52d19ab3e1ac', 'en-gy2-fler-varieteter-1', 'Engelska', 'gy2', 'Engelskans varieteter', 'Whose English?', 'En essä om accent, dialekt och jargong, med frågor om vad som sägs, hur det sägs och vad skribenten menar.', 14, 'vanlig', 'Whose English?

When I moved from Manchester to Sydney at fifteen, I thought I already spoke English. Within a week I had realised I was wrong. My classmates said ‘arvo’ for afternoon, ‘servo’ for petrol station and ‘no worries’ for almost everything. I understood the words one by one, but not the conversation.

Linguists make a useful distinction. An accent is the way a person pronounces words; a dialect also includes vocabulary and grammar. My classmates and I all had accents, then, but we also spoke different dialects. Neither was wrong, and neither was ‘better English’, although people often behave as if the version they grew up with is the real one.

Language also changes with the situation. I do not speak to my grandmother as I speak in a job interview. Many professions have their own jargon, too: words that make sense to colleagues and sound like a foreign language to everybody else.

After four years in Australia I sometimes catch myself saying ‘no worries’ in England, and my cousins laugh. I do not mind. A language is not only a place you come from. It is something you carry, and it can hold more than one home.', 'vag'),
