  ('52a7b742-ca4c-5a3f-b589-347765713ee0', 'en-gy3-fler-historia-1', 'Engelska', 'gy3', 'Engelskans historia', 'Where our words come from', 'En essä om var engelska ord kommer ifrån, med frågor om förklaringar, bildspråk och skribentens slutsats.', 10, 'vanlig', 'Where our words come from

Every English sentence is a small museum. Take the words ‘cow’ and ‘beef’. They refer to the same animal, one in the field and one on the plate, yet they come from different languages. ‘Cow’ is an old Germanic word, used by the farmers who looked after the animals. ‘Beef’ came from French after the Norman conquest of 1066, when French-speaking lords ruled England and ate the meat. The same is true of ‘sheep’ and ‘mutton’, and of ‘pig’ and ‘pork’. Historians often explain it this way, although the real story was probably more complicated.

The Vikings left their mark too. Words such as ‘egg’, ‘sky’ and ‘window’ come from Old Norse, and so do the small words ‘they’, ‘them’ and ‘their’. It is unusual for a language to borrow such everyday words, and it suggests that the Vikings and the English lived side by side and mixed.

Borrowing never stopped. English picked up ‘pyjamas’ in India, ‘ski’ from Norway and ‘tsunami’ from Japan. Some people fear that English is being damaged by new words. Linguists tend to see the opposite: a language that borrows is a language that is alive.', 'vag'),
