  ('9c08fc25-e0ce-5936-aa3a-f6a940972d67', 'en-gy3-granska-argument-2', 'Engelska', 'gy3', 'Granska argument', 'Put the screens away', 'En debattartikel om skärmar i skolan, med frågor om tes, belägg, medgivande och de argumentationsfel som skribenten gör.', 18, 'vanlig', 'Put the screens away

Last month I watched a fifteen-year-old “read” a novel for an English assignment. She scrolled through it on her phone in twenty minutes, answering messages as she went, and then told me that it was boring. I was not surprised. We have handed our children devices designed to capture their attention, and then we act shocked when they cannot concentrate.

The evidence is hard to ignore. A survey of 2,000 secondary-school pupils found that those who spent more than four hours a day on screens had lower grades than those who spent less than one hour. Screens, clearly, are making our children less intelligent.

Defenders of phones in schools seem to think that children should be allowed to do whatever they like, whenever they like, even in the middle of a maths lesson. But nobody seriously believes that a thirteen-year-old should decide his own timetable.

And where would it end? Today we allow phones in the classroom; tomorrow pupils will refuse to read anything longer than a caption, and in twenty years nobody will be able to follow a contract or a doctor’s instructions.

To be fair, screens are not all bad. Digital textbooks are often cheaper, they can read a text aloud for pupils with dyslexia, and some research suggests that short, well-designed apps can help pupils learn new words. I do not want schools to throw their laptops out of the window.

What I want is simple: phones locked away during lessons, and at least one full-length book a term read on paper. My grandparents read by candlelight and grew up to be thoughtful, patient people. Our children deserve the same chance.', 'vag'),
