  ('253989a3-376f-5f83-a59f-eba5fe278341', 'en-ak9-modal-perfekt-2', 'Engelska', 'ak9', 'Modala verb i perfekt', 'She must have forgotten', 'Att gissa vad som har hänt: must have när du är nästan säker, might have eller may have när det är möjligt, och can’t have när det inte kan stämma.', 33, 'vanlig', null, 'vag'),
  ('5aab08cd-21c9-570e-b0c9-0ce05cc9fe09', 'en-ak9-mastare-modala-verb-i-perfekt', 'Engelska', 'ak9', 'Modala verb i perfekt', 'Mästarprov: Modala verb i perfekt', 'Blandade frågor ur hela området, i ny ordning varje gång. Klarar du det med minst två stjärnor sitter området.', 34, 'mastare', null, 'vag'),
  ('1869ba6a-f21e-5f82-9798-aacc742ef604', 'en-ak9-varlden-1', 'Engelska', 'ak9', 'Engelska i världen', 'Lift or elevator?', 'Brittisk och amerikansk engelska: olika ord för samma sak, olika stavning och olika sätt att skriva datum.', 35, 'vanlig', null, 'vag'),
  ('799885dc-060f-5bd8-8989-9f36871928fc', 'en-ak9-varlden-2', 'Engelska', 'ak9', 'Engelska i världen', 'One language, many Englishes', 'En faktatext om hur engelskan spreds över världen och lever i många varianter, med frågor om vad som står och vad skribenten vill säga.', 36, 'vanlig', 'When people in Sweden think of English, many of them think of Britain or the United States. But English belongs to far more people than that. It is the first language of most people in Ireland, Australia and New Zealand, and of many people in Canada. In other countries, for example India, Nigeria and the Philippines, it is an official language that is used in schools, courts and government, even though most people there grew up speaking another language at home. In fact, more people speak English as a second or foreign language than as their first language.

How did English spread so far? One reason is history. From the 1600s onwards, Britain built a large empire, and English travelled with its ships, traders and colonial governments to many parts of the world. Later, in the 1900s, the United States became one of the most powerful countries in the world. American films, music and businesses, and later the internet, carried English even further.

Today English is often used as a lingua franca: a shared language between people who have different first languages. When a pilot from Brazil talks to air traffic control in Germany, they normally use English. When a girl from Japan and a boy from Sweden meet at a summer camp, English is often the language they choose, even though it is not the first language of either of them.

As English spread, it also changed. Every place where it is spoken has made it its own. In informal Australian English, the afternoon is often called the “arvo”, and in India and Nigeria there are words and expressions that a visitor from London might not understand at first. These varieties are not “wrong”. They are simply different, in the same way as Swedish sounds different in Skåne and in Norrland.

English has also borrowed words from all over the world. “Shampoo” comes from Hindi, “robot” from Czech, “tsunami” from Japanese and “ombudsman” from Swedish. So when you learn English, you also learn a little of many other languages.

Is there one correct English? Many language experts would say no. A pupil who learns English in Stockholm will hear many different accents in life, from Dublin to Delhi. What matters most is not sounding British or American, but understanding others and being understood.', 'vag'),
